{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>You shall keep My statutes</b> — and they are: \"You shall not let your cattle mate with a different kind\" and so forth. These statutes are decrees of the King for which there is no reason, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:19). But our Rabbis did not state that the reason should be hidden and that the evil inclination and the nations of the world would object to them — except regarding sha'atnez, not regarding the crossbreeding of animals. And the intent is not that they be a decree of the King, the King of kings, in any place without reason, for \"every word of El is pure\" (Proverbs 30:5). Rather, the statutes are the decree of the King that He enacts in His kingdom without revealing their benefit to the people, and the people do not enjoy them, but they ponder them in their hearts and accept them out of fear of the kingdom. So too the statutes of the Holy One, blessed be He, are the secrets that belong to Him in the Torah, in which the people do not partake in their understanding as they do with the mishpatim, but all of them have correct reason and complete benefit. And the reason for the prohibition of crossbreeding is that HaShem created the species in the world among all creatures possessing souls — in plants, in creatures possessing the soul of movement — and implanted in them the power of reproduction so that the species would endure through them forever, as long as He, blessed be He, desires the continuation of the world. And He charged them with the power to bring forth after their own kind, and never to change, forever, as it is stated concerning all of them: \"after its kind\" (Genesis 1). Now, He established the cause of reproduction — that one mates animals with one another for the perpetuation of species — just as men come upon women for procreation and increase. And one who crossbreeds two species alters and negates the act of Creation, as if he thinks that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not complete everything necessary in His world, and he wishes to assist in the creation of the world, to add creatures to it. And species among living creatures do not produce a species different from their own, and even those close in nature that might be born from them, such as mules — their seed will be cut off, for they do not reproduce. So from both these respects, the act of crossbreeding species is a loathsome and futile thing. Also, plants that are grafted, one species with one that is not its kind — their fruit does not grow thereafter, and their prohibition carries the two reasons mentioned above. And this is the reason for \"you shall not sow your field with two kinds of seed,\" which according to our Rabbis refers to grafting (Kiddushin 39a). And He also forbade mixed seeds, because they change in their nature and also in their form when they draw sustenance from one another, so that each seed from them would be as though it had been grafted from two species. And it is forbidden to plow with an ox and a donkey, because it is the way of everyone who works his land to bring his yoke into a single stall, and they may come to crossbreeding. And our author (Rabbeinu) adds in the reason for the prohibition of crossbreeding that it is so as not to mix the forces that cause plants to grow by drawing sustenance from one another — from what they said in Bereishit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 10:6): Rabbi Simon said, there is no herb below that does not have a mazal in the firmament above that strikes it and says to it, \"Grow!\" — as it is written (Job 38:33), \"Do you know the statutes of heaven, or do you impose their rule on earth?\" Now, one who crossbreeds mixed species or sows them in a vessel so that they draw sustenance from one another nullifies the statutes of heaven. Therefore He said concerning them, \"You shall keep My statutes,\" for they are the statutes of heaven. And thus Rabbi Chanina said in the name of Rabbi Pinchas: because of the statutes by which I decreed My world (Yerushalmi Kilayim 1:7). And I have already written in the portion of Bereishit (Genesis 1:26) that the foundation of all plants is in the supernal realms, and from there HaShem bestowed upon them the blessing of life forever, and so one who mixes crossbreeds negates and confuses the act of Creation:" "<b>Neither shall a garment of mixed stuff, sha'atnez, come upon you</b> — Why is this stated? For it is stated (Deuteronomy 22:11), \"You shall not wear sha'atnez, wool and linen together\" — one might think one should not wear carded tufts of wool and flax fibers; Scripture therefore says \"a garment.\" From where do we derive the inclusion of felt? Scripture says \"sha'atnez\" — something that is shua (combed), tavui (spun), and nuz (woven). And I say that \"nuz\" means something that is entwined and twisted, one with the other, to join them — in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Leviticus 19:19). But this does not seem correct to me, for felt is prohibited only by Rabbinic law, not by Torah law, since it is only combed, and thus we learned (Kilayim 9:9): felt is prohibited because it is combed. And in the Gemara they said (Niddah 61a): But perhaps it is either shua or tavui or nuz? And they raised the objection and the halakhah follows Mar Zutra, that the Merciful One requires them in a single expression. But the baraita taught in Torat Kohanim (Chapter 4, section 18) — \"I have only a garment; from where do I include felt? Scripture says 'sha'atnez'\" — comes to include things that are not a garment from \"You shall not wear sha'atnez,\" in which \"garment\" is not mentioned. And the midrash is primary, for even something that is not a garment is prohibited as kilayim by Torah law — such as one who plaits threads and makes from them a belt or the like — provided that it is shua and tavui and nuz. And He seized upon felt merely as an asmakhta, for it is prohibited by their words and is not a garment. And likewise what the Rav explained regarding \"shua,\" that it is something entwined and twisted one with another — and this explanation he also wrote in his commentary on the Gemara (Yevamot 5a, Niddah 61a) — they have already responded to him that his explanation does not follow from what they said (Menachot 39a): \"Learn from this that the upper knot in tzitzit is by Torah law, for if you suppose it is not by Torah law — why do I need kilayim that the Merciful One permitted in tzitzit? For we have established that a single immediate joining is not a connection.\" Now, they revealed to us that a garment made of linen species in which tzitzit of wool is tied with two knots is kilayim by Torah law, even though it is not entwined and twisted one with another. Rather, the matter is thus: Scripture said \"a garment of mixed stuff, sha'atnez\" to say that any garment made from thread that is shua and tavui and shazur (woven), one shall not wear it as kilayim — that is, as a mixture. And in the Mishneh Torah (Deuteronomy 22:11) He added to explain that one shall not wear shua, tavui, and nuz of wool and linen when they are \"together,\" meaning joined together in two joinings, to say that the wool and the linen, each of which is shua, tavui, and nuz by itself, one shall not join them together, for \"together\" is the kilayim, as He said (Deuteronomy 22:10), \"You shall not plow with an ox and a donkey together,\" meaning tied and joined. And the joining in garments is by two joinings, for from the expression \"together\" they learned that two joinings constitute a connection, and from there they learned that kilayim is prohibited even when it is not a garment, but even when one plaits the threads into cords and makes from them sashes, even though it is not woven — as in the matter we learned (Kilayim 9:9): one may not tie a band of wool with one of linen to gird oneself with it. And the word \"nuz\" is an abbreviated word in this composite term, just as \"tavui\" contributes only the letter tet to it. And its meaning, in my opinion, is naloz, from the expression \"an abomination to YHWH is one who is naloz\" (Proverbs 3:32) — one who turns aside and perverts his ways — \"and those who are nalozim in their paths\" (Proverbs 2:15) — twisted and turning, for so is a woven thing twisted and bent. And as we learned (Kilayim 9:8): Rabbi Shimon says: \"naloz and maliz — his Father in heaven acts against him\" — the honorable Name turns away from its accustomed way of mercy upon him, as it is said, \"and with the crooked You act perversely\" (Psalms 18:27). And in the language of the Sages it is used concisely: a prince's son whose heart was naloz against him, and he took the claw to burrow against his father (Shemot Rabbah 37:2) — his heart turned toward an evil path. And from this they said in the Talmud of Sanhedrin (41a): \"from your nazyateih you would not have said anything at all\" — meaning, if you wanted to twist and turn matters, we would know nothing against you — \"nazyateih\" like \"nalzyateih,\" meaning your turning aside. And this is the truth and the correct view in this matter. And therefore it was necessary to permit kilayim in tzitzit even though the tzitzit is not woven and is not a garment. And this is Torah law. But the Sages prohibited even that which is made with only one of them — whether shua, tavui, or nuz — and therefore we learned (Kilayim 9:9): felt is prohibited because it is combed; a tip of wool with a tip of linen is prohibited because one girds with them as a woven thing — and all of this is by Rabbinic law. And the reason for kilayim of garments is to keep mixtures of species far away, and He prohibited the usual practice of making a garment from them. And the Rav said in the Guide for the Perplexed (III:37) that the reason for sha'atnez is that in those times there was a well-known garment worn by the priests of sorcerers, in which they performed all their acts. And he said that he found this written in their books. And because this was a great and very desirable matter for them, that they would perform in it their known acts for idolatry and for demons, the Torah distances it from every person, for the Torah comes to obliterate their deeds and to eradicate their memory:"], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "The reference to 'our author' (ומחברינו) in the middle of the comment likely refers to Rabbeinu Tam or a similar Ba'alei ha-Tosafot figure, based on the argument attributed to him; resolved by context as the French school of commentators.", 1 "The phrase 'מִנַּיִן לְרַבּוֹת אֶת הַלְּבָדִים' introduces felt; the abbreviation הקב\"ה for הקדוש ברוך הוא (the Holy One, blessed be He) is rendered throughout per the standard reverent register without substitution."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:57:05.130215566Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}